#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120653} :activity/id #uuid "288fdc58-d951-55d7-87ce-92acf93dd2ce" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048743 :topic/id #uuid "ba8c9515-59e3-55c9-9d86-1de6e55c5474" :topic/math-academy-id 1779} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121028 :tutorial/content "In the same way that we can factor numbers or polynomials, we can factor a matrix by writing it as a product of two or more matrices. In fact, there is one factorization technique that works for *all* matrices, called **LU factorization** (or **LU decomposition**).\n\nLU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- $[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]$ is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, we can write the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ in LU factored form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou may be wondering how we arrived at this factorization. Shortly, we'll discuss an algorithm that explains how to compute $L$ and $U$ for any $2\\times2$ matrix $A.$ But for now, let's focus on recognizing LU factored forms." :tutorial/id #uuid "827a0fea-203f-5c8c-bb6a-4d0764f6de04" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3176 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cca0ee93-f5ae-5db9-98de-167dafd23a32" :step/math-academy-id 17355 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119603 :question/id #uuid "0e45ac96-0eee-51a3-83e0-192582dcf57d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4765"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048373}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402054}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "9ff11c4f-9a18-5d14-b5c7-e64d00a7d65b" :step/math-academy-id 17356 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119604 :question/id #uuid "e1d1f80c-70d1-566d-9cc0-94899b0fa05d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4767"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399037}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "bb1d2bfc-7ab9-5e32-ba3e-2e7219f0bcc9" :step/math-academy-id 17359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119668 :question/id #uuid "ea39d5ec-9c8f-572d-8708-fdd97d0c04bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-6184"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050283}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345033}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"} :step/id #uuid "ccd98e02-0c2a-5392-995e-e13e8079f540" :step/math-academy-id 23125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120758}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120758 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121067 :tutorial/content "Let $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** To get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n- we can only add a multiple of the $1$st row to the $2$nd row ($R_2:=R_2+k R_1$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." :tutorial/id #uuid "4f9a2842-c4f1-577c-827c-41e33f514c31" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3977 :tutorial/title "An Algorithm for Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e8257db3-4571-507c-aee8-af086a0be8da" :step/math-academy-id 23628 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120654}}] :activity/title "LU Factorization of 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120001}]}
